We have a need to include things inside other things that build up using "custom" interpreters.
The driving example of this is the "Fields" tag inside dynamodb, which is nothing like an ordinary interpreter.
We could reductively say "everything is a map", but then how are we not the same as JSON or YAML?
That is not why we are here.

Equally, we could say "implement a special command", but that doesn't feel like it is the most natural thing for users,
or the easiest thing for developers.

So we introduce another "property" syntax: a <= I, which says "there is a property a whose value can be determined by examining
all nested lines using an instance of interpreter I".

I must be a symbol, and it must resolve to an Interpreter.

	target usenamed

		ensure test.DB.Table "a-table"
			@teardown delete

I haven't drifted that far from what dynamo does, but I have used my own syntax for testing simplicity.

			Fields <= test.FieldInterpreter
				Key hello string
				Value world number
